hi steve coffin here uh  i'm gonna do another video  right behind the one that i just did  about listening versus speaking because  there's been a  quite a bit of reaction both at my blog  the linguist on language blog  and at youtube  and uh  the original video that i put up was  about why i tend to favor listening  as the sort of area where we should  spend most of our time in learning a  language listening and reading in other  words input activities rather than  speaking and so i had a variety of  reactions and i'm going to i've got them  listed out here and if i can read my  writing i'm going to comment on them all  um  the first one  was first of all i will preface um  everything that i'm going to say  by pointing out that to me the the  fundamental task in language learning is  to acquire words and that's why at link  lingq.com we measure the number of words  you know  and to me knowing a word means  understanding it it's it's perfectly  normal that we should have a much larger  passive vocabulary than active  vocabulary it's that's good because we  need to understand what people say to us  we can always use a more limited  vocabulary to express our own thoughts  but if we don't understand the words  that people are using  in our reading or our listening then we  are going to have a very tough time  so passive vocabulary that's the key  that's what i like to build up and i  know that eventually some of these will  convert into active words and probably i  need to use them for them uh to become  active also some people say well it's  not words it's phrases or it's chunks  perhaps and i believe in that i think we  need to learn chunks of the language we  need to learn learn phrases  uh but phrases are consist of words so  it still boils down to words counting  the number of words that you  know  i'm not a big believer in the idea of  saving a bunch of sentences because to  me every sentence is different unique i  like  i do like studying phrases but whether  it be sentences which some people prefer  or phrases we're talking about words you  need to know words  so  uh now some of the issues that came up  peop some people  like the idea of speaking from the  beginning and there are a number of  people on the internet who have quite a  a following like moses mccormick  uh and of course benny  and now vince here at street smart  uh and there are uh other learners who  tell me that they really like  you know systems like pimsleur or  michelle thomas because they get you  speaking early  personally  i don't think it's very important to  speak early what matters is how soon do  you  reach a level that i would call fluency  because that's my goal typically fluency  i want to get there as quickly as i can  fluency does not mean being able to say  hello how are you where's the bathroom  can i have a beer  or may i have this dance although those  are useful things to know how to do and  you can get there pretty quickly  fluency to me is the ability to express  yourself comfortably  on a number of subjects in particular  subjects that you're familiar with  i can't discuss  automobile mechanics in a number of  languages but most of the subjects that  i'm familiar with  i can discuss in the language that i  languages that i claim to be fluent in  to become fluent you need to have a lot  of words because fluent implies  conversation dialogue it implies  understanding what the other person is  saying  so  whether after one or two or three months  i can say something in the new language  doesn't really matter to me  obviously if i'm in a place where native  speakers of that language are readily  available i will attack them with  whatever i have  and just for fun because it's fun we  want to try stuff out but the  vast majority of my learning time and  this was the case when i lived in japan  was spent listening and reading and  building up up my vocabulary so that i  can understand what people say to me  so  those people who are more concerned  about speaking early they will like  pimsleur  or michelle thomas because they get you  saying things whether that  takes you to where you're going to be  fluent is another question  i think a lot of people are  uncomfortable being in that sort of  situation of uncertainty in the language  it's not clear it's i can't pronounce it  properly i make mistakes i can't express  myself  uh  and so therefore they they want to have  a little more certainty  and i saw one commenter kate  said that she's going to take lessons  because she's been listening and she  still can't say very much in french so  she's going to go to a tutor  to me if i followed her argument or not  her points and then damien came in with  some suggestions of various sources of  of learner content  and damian said he was on nasimil for  six months and kate said she'd been  listening for i don't know how many  months but many many months and damien  recommended you know  various learner items well  me personally after two months i don't  want to be on learner content after two  months i want to be on stuff of interest  and challenging myself and listening  repeatedly if necessarily and necessary  and everything that i listened to victor  said well how do you learn if you just  listen well yeah everything you listen  to you've got to read and therefore  until i am very good i can't access  podcasts for where for which there are  no transcripts and that's why at link we  all of our content has both audio and  transcripts and then i worked the  transcripts in the old days i used to  use readers with wordless now i use on  link our dictionaries and i save the  words and review them in flashcards so  that the listening that i'm listening to  now for enjoyment and out of interest  after the first couple of months that's  what's driving me to increase my  vocabulary a couple of other comments  shadowing i don't do shadowing a lot of  people swear by it that's fine and  people have their own ways of learning i  listen because i find the subject  interesting the first two months i'm  forced to listen to less interesting  content although we have at link some  beginner content that's paralleled in  all 10 languages and i spent a good part  of two months listening to who is she a  story in 26 episodes in russian where i  knew what the story was in english and i  saved words and phrases so the first two  months you're listening to boring stuff  but beyond that i'm listening for  meaning so i'm not interested in  shadowing i'm listening for meaning and  then i'm reading and then i'm saving the  words and reviewing them in my  flashcards another person asked do you  vocalize yes i vocalize sub-vocalize uh  in foreign languages i think we all do  it until we get so fluent in that  language that we can read it like our  own and so  the advantage of having the audio before  you read something is that you've got  some momentum going into the reading you  know roughly what it's about  uh and you it's easier than to sub  vocalize you've got that rhythm going  and so you're trying to read up to that  rhythm the rhythm that you were  listening to so yes i sub-vocalize and i  benefit a lot from having listened to  something beforehand  one person said how much russian do you  understand after three years well  basically i understand it all if i know  the words but they're still  according to the link five ten percent  of words that are new to me but i i know  where the word begins and ends i may not  know every word so i'm constantly  working to improve my vocabulary but  most of the interviews at ekko muskri  if i pick up tolstoy i have no trouble  reading him if i pick up  an author that i'm less familiar with i  will encounter more unfamiliar  vocabulary and so it'll be more  difficult um somebody said where are the  beginner resources um you got to look  for them but we have a lot at link so  you can try out link and it's all audio  and text  somebody said where do you find  